Q: C# WPF timer (stopwatch) with DispatcherTimer A: <p>such works:</p>
<pre><code>using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;
using System.Windows;
using System.Windows.Controls;
using System.Windows.Data;
using System.Windows.Documents;
using System.Windows.Input;
using System.Windows.Media;

using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Windows.Threading; // Required namespace

namespace WpfClockApp
{
    /// &lt;summary&gt;
    /// Interaction logic for Window1.xaml
    /// &lt;/summary&gt;
    public partial class Window1 : Window
    {
        private DispatcherTimer _dt = new DispatcherTimer();        
        private Stopwatch _sw = new Stopwatch();

        public Window1()
        {
            InitializeComponent();           
        }

        private void Dt_Tick(object sender, EventArgs e)
        {
                timeLabel.Content = DateTime.Now.ToString();
        }

        private void button1_Click(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
        {                           
                _dt.Tick += Dt_Tick;
                _dt.Interval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1);
                _dt.Start();
                _sw.Start();
        }


        private void button2_Click(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
        {
            _sw.Stop();
            _dt.Stop();         
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>for XAML:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;?&gt;
&lt;Window
    x:Class=&quot;WpfClockApp.Window1&quot; xmlns=&quot;http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation&quot; xmlns:x=&quot;http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml&quot;
    Title=&quot;WPF Clock&quot;
    Height=&quot;300&quot;
    Width=&quot;300&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Grid&gt;
        &lt;Label
            Width=&quot;144&quot;
            Height=&quot;29&quot;
            Grid.Column=&quot;0&quot;
            Grid.Row=&quot;0&quot;
            HorizontalAlignment=&quot;Left&quot;
            VerticalAlignment=&quot;Top&quot;
            Margin=&quot;21,25,0,0&quot;
            x:Name=&quot;timeLabel&quot;
            BorderBrush=&quot;#FFA5A2A2&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;Button
            x:Name=&quot;button1&quot;
            Click=&quot;button1_Click&quot;
            RenderTransformOrigin=&quot;0.5,0.5&quot;
            Height=&quot;23&quot;
            Width=&quot;75&quot;
            Margin=&quot;30,91,0,0&quot;
            VerticalAlignment=&quot;Top&quot;
            HorizontalAlignment=&quot;Left&quot;
            Grid.Row=&quot;0&quot;
            Grid.Column=&quot;0&quot;
            Content=&quot;Start&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;Button
            Width=&quot;75&quot;
            Height=&quot;23&quot;
            x:Name=&quot;button2&quot;
            Click=&quot;button2_Click&quot;
            Grid.Column=&quot;0&quot;
            Grid.Row=&quot;0&quot;
            HorizontalAlignment=&quot;Left&quot;
            VerticalAlignment=&quot;Top&quot;
            Margin=&quot;30,141,0,0&quot;
            RenderTransformOrigin=&quot;0.5,0.5&quot;
            Content=&quot;Stop&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/Grid&gt;
&lt;/Window&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: POST unchecked HTML checkboxes A: <p>Over 30 answers - with solutions of varying degrees of complexity - but the key question remains: <strong>In what situation is this problem even relevant?</strong> I have discovered that <strong>there is only one situation</strong> (which I like to describe here). But there is really an extremely simple solution without JavaScript, etc. which I like to introduce to you further below. First:</p>
<h3>Normally, it is okay to just consider server side a status as &quot;false&quot; as long no checkbox value was transmitted via URL parameter - finito, basta!</h3>
<p><strong>...that's what I thought</strong> until I ran into the following problem:</p>
<p>If a input form is generated dynamically by the server using predefined default value(s), changes by the user from &quot;true&quot; to &quot;false&quot; will not be recognized. Here, how it works (wrong):</p>
<p>First, the form is requested from the server via URL without parameters and therefore returned with a server side default value. Assuming the default value is &quot;true&quot;, the corresponding checkbox on the form would be activated too. If the user now deactivates this checkbox (because he wants to change the default value to &quot;false&quot;), after submitting the form ...nothing happens!!</p>
<p>Why? Because the checkbox is disabled, the form is submitted again without URL parameters, so the server continues to use his default value (in this example &quot;true&quot;). This means that the user has absolutely no chance of overwriting the default value with &quot;false&quot;! <strong>Only this (or a comparable) situation poses a real problem</strong> - everything else can be solved with the condition &quot;checkbox transmitted&quot; resp. &quot;not transmitted&quot; (see my headline above).</p>
<p>However - my special problem can also be solved very easy: There is only one additional, hidden field (e.g. named &quot;reloaded&quot; with any value, e.g., &quot;1&quot;) needed somewhere within the input form.</p>
<p>The processing script on the server now must check whether the parameter &quot;reloaded&quot; is included in the URL. When a &quot;virgin&quot; form is requested for the first time, no transfer parameters are transmitted and the server returns it with its default value (as described above). If the user has made any changes to the form, the server also receives the value of the field &quot;reloaded&quot;. The presence of this field parameter must now cause the server to treat all non-mentioned checkboxes as &quot;false&quot; and overwrite the relevant default values accordingly - <strong>that's it!</strong></p>
<p>Example snippet:</p>
<pre><code>// This happens on server side (PHP):
// (Note: You can use as many default settings and checkboxes as you like but
// the script can be shortened substantially by using arrays in this case!)

// The server defined default value:
$setting = true;

// A substitute value if checkbox parameter missing:
// (must be defined separately for each setting, so its
// advisable using an array if there are multiple settings.)
$zerostate = $setting;

// If parameter &quot;reloaded&quot; (comming from the form) existe, change the substitute:
if(isset($_GET['reloaded')) $zerostate = false;

// Get the returned checkbox state:
if(isset($_GET['boxname')) $setting = true;
else $setting = $zerostate;
</code></pre>
<p>... so simple!</p>
